---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";

const metrics = [
  { value: "63%", label: "Decline in quarterly conversion" },
  { value: "€4.7M", label: "Year-1 net benefit" },
  { value: "46.9%", label: "Year-1 ROI" },
  { value: "7.4x", label: "LTV to CAC, Year 1" },
];

const links = [
  { label: "Deck (PDF)", href: "/media/Linh_Le_Compass_AI_Spotify_Conversion.pdf" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="The best next action is sometimes to say nothing at all"
  description="Spotify's free-to-paid conversion fell 63% while its margins improved. Compass AI, a behavioural decision layer that scores silence as a candidate action. Case study by Linh (Min) Le."
  project="Compass AI, Spotify"
  period="July 2026"
  tint="finance"
  hook="Spotify repaired its income statement by starving the engine that fills it, and the repair looked like a turnaround right up until you separated the two things it was doing at once."
  metrics={metrics}
  links={links}
>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The decision this informed</h2>
        <p>
          Sixty-two percent of Spotify's monthly listeners, some 468 million people, sit on the
          free tier. The company has said publicly that it wants a billion users by 2030 with the
          majority of them still free, which rules out the easiest way to push people toward
          Premium, namely making the free experience worse. The question on the table at the Viet
          Career Conference Think-A-Thon was how to convert more of that base without touching it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our team's deliverable was an investment recommendation with a number attached: how much
          to commit, against what return, and under which conditions to stop. I led the quantitative
          work, which meant the diagnosis, the financial model, and the measurement design that would
          have to hold up if anyone actually spent the money.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The finding</h2>
        <p>
          Between 2019 and 2026 the rate at which free listeners became paying subscribers fell from
          <strong>4.42% per quarter to 1.64%</strong>, a <strong>63% decline</strong> that a simple
          time regression explains with an R&sup2; of 0.78 at t = &minus;8.18. That is a structural
          drift rather than a noisy quarter, and it ran underneath one of the most celebrated margin
          turnarounds in the company's history: operating margin moved from
          <strong>&minus;5.1% to +15.8%</strong> across the same window, a 20.9 point swing.
        </p>
        <Figure
          src="/figures/compass-conversion-decline.png"
          alt="Time series chart of Spotify's quarterly free-to-paid conversion rate, declining steadily across the period covered by the analysis."
          caption="Quarterly conversion flow across the panel. The decline is steady enough that time alone explains 78% of the movement."
        />
        <p>
          The two facts are connected, and the connection runs through sales and marketing. Marketing
          spend fell from <strong>12.0% to 7.5% of revenue</strong> while the new-signup rate fell
          from 15.1% to 7.5% per quarter, the two moving together at a correlation of 0.69. Roughly
          <strong>63% of the margin improvement</strong> came from a one-time cost reset, which is a
          lever that works exactly once.
        </p>
        <p>
          The instinct in a room looking at falling conversion is to blame churn. Splitting the
          decline apart showed cancellations improving over the period, from 9.4% down to 7.3% of the
          paid base. Strip that cushion away and the acquisition weakness is worse than the headline
          suggests, at <strong>&minus;7.65 points against a &minus;5.16 point net decline</strong>.
          Every part of the deterioration traces to people no longer starting to pay.
        </p>
        <p>
          That reframes the entire investment case. A retention problem is solved with offers and
          win-back campaigns. An acquisition problem inside a base of 468 million people is a
          targeting problem, and targeting problems are won by deciding who to leave alone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">What I did, and the obvious alternative I rejected</h2>
        <p>
          The first rejected alternative was a metric, and catching it changed every number
          downstream. The intuitive way to measure free-to-paid conversion is subscribers divided by
          monthly active users, and an early pass of this analysis used exactly that. It is wrong in
          a specific and dangerous way, because it falls whenever the free base grows regardless of
          how the funnel is performing, which makes healthy top-of-funnel growth look like decline.
          Spotify's own reporting already carried a computed conversion rate, on a row the first pass
          had skipped. The analysis was rebuilt on the source definition, and every sheet read now
          resolves by row label instead of row position so the same mistake cannot recur silently.
        </p>
        <div class="pull">
          The published figures come from net conversion flow, meaning net new paying subscribers in
          a quarter over the free base that existed at the end of the previous one. That is a lever
          management can pull. Subscribers over monthly actives is an outcome that moves on its own.
        </div>
        <p>
          The second rejected alternative was the set of levers everyone reaches for first. Cutting
          price erodes the 34.8% Premium gross margin that the 2030 plan depends on, against roughly
          13% on the ad-supported side. Adding features is expensive, copyable, and subject to
          diminishing returns as users adapt. Degrading the free tier reduces sign-ups and works
          directly against the company's own stated free-majority target. Each of those moves the
          product or the price for everyone, which is the wrong shape of instrument for a problem
          where users differ from one another.
        </p>
        <p>
          What survived was a decision layer rather than an offer. Compass AI sorts the eligible base
          by whether an intervention would actually change the outcome, which produces four groups
          and only one worth spending on. Users who would upgrade anyway cost money to convince.
          Users who will never upgrade cost money to annoy. Users already dismissing promotions get
          made worse by another one. The engine acts only on people whose decision it can move, and
          it treats staying silent as a fifth candidate action that every other option has to beat on
          expected value after costs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Building the evidence meant a 32-quarter financial database assembled directly from
          Spotify's SEC filings, covering Q2 2018 through Q1 2026, with 64 dated events mapped to the
          quarter each one landed in. An event-study model then tested whether price changes, feature
          launches or acquisitions explained the conversion decline. None of them did to any
          statistically meaningful degree, which is what moved the diagnosis from product timeline to
          marketing spend.
        </p>
        <p>
          The financial model runs off a single assumptions tab, and every figure quoted anywhere in
          the deck reconciles to it. Base case is a 40% persuadable share of the eligible pool, 90%
          in-app delivery reach, and a 2.0 point conditional lift, producing about 758,000 incremental
          conversions worth <strong>€14.76M</strong> in gross profit against <strong>€10.04M</strong>
          of first-year cost. That leaves <strong>€4.7M</strong> of net benefit at
          <strong>46.9%</strong> return, a <strong>6.7 month</strong> payback on the €6.0M build, and
          a three-year net present value of <strong>€16.34M</strong> discounted at 10%, which is a
          deliberate hurdle rate set well above Spotify's actual cost of capital so the number errs
          low.
        </p>
        <Figure
          src="/figures/compass-business-impact-range.png"
          alt="Chart showing the range of modelled Year-1 business impact for Compass AI across scenarios."
          caption="Year-1 impact across the modelled range. The base case sits inside a band rather than at a point, because three of the inputs are assumptions awaiting a pilot."
        />
        <p>
          The part I would defend hardest is the stopping rule. Each load-bearing assumption carries a
          published break-even and a cushion: the lift can fall to 1.33 points, the persuadable share
          to 26.6%, and reach to 59.8% before the case stops clearing, each roughly a 1.5x margin.
          Six pre-committed triggers state in advance what would falsify the recommendation and what
          happens when one fires, including pausing spend if measured lift sits below break-even for
          two consecutive quarters. Writing those down before the pilot runs is what separates an
          investment case from a pitch.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <header class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
        <h2 class="h-sub">The submitted deck</h2>
        <p class="section-note">
          Twenty-three pages, including the appendix carrying the conversion build, the unit
          economics, the break-even sensitivities and the experiment design.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="embed">
        <iframe src="/media/Linh_Le_Compass_AI_Spotify_Conversion.pdf"
                title="Compass AI submitted deck" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      </div>
      <p class="embed-note">
        <a href="/media/Linh_Le_Compass_AI_Spotify_Conversion.pdf">Open the deck in its own tab</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">Assumptions and limitations</h2>
        <p>
          Compass AI was designed and modelled. It was never built and never deployed, so every
          financial figure on this page is a projection under stated assumptions. The measured
          outcome is the second-place finish.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three numbers carry the case and all three are assumptions awaiting a pilot. The 40%
          persuadable share, the 90% delivery reach and the 2.0 point conditional lift were chosen as
          conservative mid-cases and calibrated against published benchmarks, with self-serve freemium
          free-to-paid conversion running 3 to 5 percent overall and mature personalisation programmes
          showing double-digit lifts against low-maturity peers. They remain the model's most
          sensitive inputs.
        </p>
        <p>
          The segmentation rests on a simplifying step that the deck labels as such. Treating the
          high-engagement, high-friction quadrant as 25% of the free base assumes an even distribution
          across a two-by-two screen, and it is a modelling convenience rather than an observed
          Spotify statistic. A pilot should replace it with the real joint distribution.
        </p>
        <p>
          The cancellation rate is anchored to Spotify's own disclosed figure of roughly 4% monthly
          from 2020, which sits inside the range the data cannot rule out. That anchoring pins down
          the shape of the new-signup decline with confidence while leaving the cancellation level
          itself an informed estimate.
        </p>
        <p>
          The link between marketing spend and the signup decline is a correlation of 0.69 across the
          panel. It is the simplest available explanation for a decline the event model cannot account
          for, and it is not proof of causation. Establishing that would need a country-level
          breakdown, which public filings do not support.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
